{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t02-008",
  "track": "track-02",
  "title": "ASP.NET Core configuration providers binding and options lifecycle",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "inventoryComplete": true,
  "concepts": [
    "Configuration is a hierarchical key/value view assembled from ordered providers. Later providers normally override earlier providers for the same key, so environment-specific and command-line values can override defaults without changing application code.",
    "Configuration providers load external representations into the common IConfiguration abstraction; the abstraction does not make every provider reloadable or every value strongly typed. Binding converts configuration values into application types and can fail or silently leave defaults depending on the binding path and settings.",
    "The options pattern gives a settings type a named configuration, post-configuration, validation, and controlled lifetime. IOptions is a cached startup value, IOptionsSnapshot recomputes per scope, and IOptionsMonitor is singleton-safe and supports change notifications where the source provides them.",
    "Treat configuration as untrusted input: validate required values and ranges, keep secrets out of committed files and logs, and understand which source wins. Reload is a provider capability with a consistency and notification boundary, not a guarantee that all settings change atomically everywhere."
  ],
